{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "def largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    '''\n    Create a function that returns a tuple (a, b), where 'a' is\n    the largest of negative integers, and 'b' is the smallest\n    of positive integers in a list.\n    '''\n    if not lst or all(x == 0 for x in lst):\n        return (None, None)\n\n    negative_integers = [x for x in lst if x < 0]\n    positive_integers = [x for x in lst if x > 0]\n\n    largest_negative = max(negative_integers) if negative_integers else None\n    smallest_positive = min(positive_integers) if positive_integers else None\n\n    return (largest_negative, smallest_positive)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    '''\n    Create a function that returns a tuple (a, b), where 'a' is\n    the largest of negative integers, and 'b' is the smallest\n    of positive integers in a list.\n    If there is no negative or positive integers, return them as None.\n    '''\n    largest_neg = None\n    smallest_pos = None\n\n    # Handle empty list or list containing only zero as per design.\n    if not lst or (len(lst) == 1 and lst[0] == 0):\n        return (None, None)\n\n    for item in lst:\n        if item < 0:\n            # Update largest_neg if current item is larger or it's the first negative found.\n            if largest_neg is None or item > largest_neg:\n                largest_neg = item\n        elif item > 0:\n            # Update smallest_pos if current item is smaller or it's the first positive found.\n            if smallest_pos is None or item < smallest_pos:\n                smallest_pos = item\n\n    return (largest_neg, smallest_pos)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a string of square brackets contains a nested subsequence\n    by tracking bracket depth and a nested segment flag.\n    \"\"\"\n    open_count = 0\n    has_double_open_segment = False\n\n    for char in string:\n        if char == '[':\n            open_count += 1\n            if open_count >= 2:\n                has_double_open_segment = True\n        elif char == ']':\n            if open_count > 0:\n                open_count -= 1\n            else:\n                # Reset has_double_open_segment if an unmatched closing bracket is found.\n                has_double_open_segment = False\n\n    return has_double_open_segment\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "```python\ndef _normalize_to_float(value):\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert various input types to float, handling string representations with comma decimal separators.\n    Directly cast for int/float, replace ',' with '.' then cast for strings.\n    \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, str):\n        value = value.replace(',', '.')\n    return float(value)\n\ndef compare_one(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Create a function that takes integers, floats, or strings representing\n    real numbers, and returns the larger variable in its given variable type.\n    Return None if the values are equal.\n    Note: If a real number is represented as a string, the floating point might be . or ,\n\n    compare_one(1, 2.5) \u279e 2.5\n    compare_one(1, \"2,3\") \u279e \"2,3\"\n    compare_one(\"5,1\", \"6\") \u279e \"6\"\n    compare_one(\"1\", 1) \u279e None\n    \"\"\"\n    a_float = _normalize_to_float(a)\n    b_float = _normalize_to_float(b)\n\n    if a_float == b_float:\n        return None\n    elif a_float > b_float:\n        return a\n    else:\n        return b\n```\n"}
